item.text:
Reused evidence
No prior chapter evidence cleanly carries over to Chapter 5B for 2022, so this pass uses new evidence only.

New evidence
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with India","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/12/19/pr22444-india-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation","claim":"IMF said India’s authorities responded to post-pandemic shocks with fiscal policy measures to support vulnerable groups and with front-loaded monetary tightening to address elevated inflation.","locator":"lines 244-245 and 251-253","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary IMF Article IV release with explicit assessment of policy response and fiscal/monetary actions in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about FY2022/23 and the 2022 policy year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Modi-led Union government, though the monetary tightening was executed by the RBI rather than Modi personally.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF also warned that inflation remained high and that public debt risks had increased."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with India","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/12/19/pr22444-india-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation","claim":"IMF credited India’s digital public infrastructure with rapid deployment of support, efficient payments, and increased financial inclusion, while saying narrowings of the digital divide, female labor force participation, youth unemployment, and informality remained important issues.","locator":"lines 255-256","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same IMF release, but this claim is distinct because it covers digital infrastructure and distributional labor-market weaknesses.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 economic governance and distribution.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Modi-era policy architecture, but digital infrastructure was built through multiple ministries and prior years.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF explicitly said female labor force participation, youth unemployment, and informality still needed progress."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Approves $750 Million Loan to India to Catalyze Private Financing and Support Economic Growth","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/29/world-bank-approves-750-million-loan-to-india-to-catalyze-private-financing-and-support-economic-growth","claim":"The World Bank approved a $750 million DPL to support reforms that leverage private sector investment in infrastructure, small businesses, and green finance markets.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'WASHINGTON, June 29, 2022'","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release describing the exact policy loan and reform package.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Government of India under Modi, though the loan reflects lender endorsement of a broader reform package.","contrary_evidence":["The page also notes large infrastructure and MSME finance gaps, so the loan addresses a shortfall rather than proving prior success."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Approves $750 Million Loan to India to Catalyze Private Financing and Support Economic Growth","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/29/world-bank-approves-750-million-loan-to-india-to-catalyze-private-financing-and-support-economic-growth","claim":"The operation supported issuing India’s first sovereign green bonds and improving access to credit for MSMEs and women entrepreneurs through de-risking and guarantee mechanisms.","locator":"bullet points 'Developing the markets for green finance' and 'Improving access to credit for MSMEs and women entrepreneurs'","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary lender description of the supported reforms and target beneficiaries.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 reform design and implementation support.","ruler_attribution":"Government-level attribution under Modi, with implementation shared across finance, banking, and sector ministries.","contrary_evidence":["The same source frames these as reforms to fill financing gaps, so it does not show that access problems were already solved."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6","5B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India Better Positioned to Navigate Global Headwinds Than Other Major Emerging Economies: New World Bank Report","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-06","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/05/india-better-positioned-to-navigate-global-headwinds-than-other-major-emerging-economies-new-world-bank-report","claim":"The World Bank said India’s economy was resilient and relatively well positioned to weather global spillovers compared with most emerging markets, despite a deteriorating external environment.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'NEW DELHI, 06 December 2022'","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank report summary with an explicit macro assessment for late 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 macro year and growth outlook.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Modi’s government, but the report also emphasizes external shocks and structural conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The same report says the external environment would still weigh on growth prospects and require continued vigilance."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India Better Positioned to Navigate Global Headwinds Than Other Major Emerging Economies: New World Bank Report","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-06","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/05/india-better-positioned-to-navigate-global-headwinds-than-other-major-emerging-economies-new-world-bank-report","claim":"The World Bank said policy reforms and prudent regulatory measures improved resilience, while market borrowings improved transparency and credibility of fiscal policy and inflation targeting helped monetary credibility.","locator":"paragraph beginning 'Policy reforms and prudent regulatory measures have also played a key role'","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank assessment with specific mechanisms named.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 policy posture.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly attributable to the Modi government and the RBI framework, but not to one office-holder alone.","contrary_evidence":["The same passage still implies continued macro risks and a need for caution."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Union Budget 2022-23 Analysis","publisher":"PRS Legislative Research","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://prsindia.org/budgets/parliament/union-budget-2022-23-analysis","claim":"PRS summarized the 2022-23 Union Budget as raising capital expenditure by 24.5 percent and setting the fiscal deficit at 6.4 percent of GDP.","locator":"lines 341-342 and 1341-1343","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent budget analysis grounded in official budget documents with exact fiscal figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022 budget policy for FY2022/23.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Modi government through the Union Budget, though execution depended on ministries and state uptake.","contrary_evidence":["A 6.4 percent fiscal deficit is still a large deficit, so this is not simple prudence without qualification."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Union Budget 2022-23 Analysis","publisher":"PRS Legislative Research","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://prsindia.org/budgets/parliament/union-budget-2022-23-analysis","claim":"PRS reported larger allocations for Road Transport and Highways, Education, Health and Family Welfare, Jal Shakti, PM-KISAN, MGNREGS, National Education Mission, National Health Mission, and welfare of women and children in the 2022-23 budget.","locator":"lines 821-831, 904-938, 1087-1168, and 1285-1303","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent budget analysis of sector and scheme allocations with exact numbers.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022 budget allocations affecting households and productivity.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Modi government’s budget priorities, though spending outcomes depend on implementation by line ministries and states.","contrary_evidence":["Some other social allocations were flat or fell, so the budget mix was selective rather than uniformly expansionary."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Union Budget 2022-23 Analysis","publisher":"PRS Legislative Research","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://prsindia.org/budgets/parliament/union-budget-2022-23-analysis","claim":"PRS noted that the budget linked transport and logistics projects to PM GatiShakti, created the DESH Stack for skilling and job matching, and planned sovereign green bonds for green infrastructure.","locator":"lines 121-127","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent budget analysis summarizing multiple named policy instruments in one budget package.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022 policy design.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Modi government’s economic agenda, with operational delivery spread across ministries.","contrary_evidence":["These were plans and framework choices; the source does not itself prove successful execution in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Centre extends Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana (PMGKAY) for another six months (April-September, 2022)","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-03-26","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1810048&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The Union Cabinet chaired by Prime Minister Narendra Modi extended PMGKAY for another six months to September 2022, adding free foodgrains for poor and vulnerable households.","locator":"opening paragraph and scheme summary in the PIB release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Cabinet release announcing the extension and naming Modi as the chair of the Cabinet decision.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Modi as Cabinet chair.","contrary_evidence":["The scheme was an extension of a pandemic-era program, so it reflects continuation as much as new initiative."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Centre extends Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Ann Yojana (PMGKAY) for another three months (October 2022-December 2022)","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1862944","claim":"The Union Cabinet approved another extension of PMGKAY for October to December 2022, providing five kilograms of free grain per person per month to NFSA beneficiaries.","locator":"opening paragraph and scheme summary in the PIB release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official Cabinet release with explicit coverage of the 2022 extension.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Modi as Cabinet chair.","contrary_evidence":["Again, this is continuation of an existing safety-net rather than evidence of a new structural reform."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Study to Assess the Impact of MGNREGS","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1810967&lang=2&reg=3","claim":"The Ministry of Rural Development said that in FY2021-22 MGNREGS had completed 83.82 lakh works and had 133.09 lakh works ongoing, while remaining a demand-driven wage-employment program.","locator":"opening lines and FY2021-22 performance summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry release with concrete implementation statistics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the fiscal year ending in 2022 and thus the target year period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Modi-led central government, though state execution is crucial for delivery.","contrary_evidence":["The release is descriptive of works and does not show how well wages or job quality translated into long-term gains."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"₹1,40,885 crore gross GST Revenue collection for May 2022; increase of 44% year-on-year","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-06-01","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleaseIframePage.aspx?LID=1&PRID=1830039&RegID=3","claim":"The government reported gross GST revenue of ₹1,40,885 crore for May 2022, up 44 percent year-on-year and one of the highest monthly collections since GST began.","locator":"headline and opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official fiscal release with exact monthly tax-collection data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022 fiscal performance.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Modi government’s tax administration and the recovery environment, but not uniquely to one policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["A single strong tax month does not prove broad household welfare or fair distribution."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Centre Clears Entire GST Compensation Due Till Date (31ST MAY, 2022)","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-05-31","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1829777&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The central government released ₹86,912 crore in GST compensation to states to help them manage resources and continue capital expenditure.","locator":"opening paragraphs and table of state-wise breakup","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official central-government fiscal release with a clear statement of funds transferred to states.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi-led central government’s fiscal choices.","contrary_evidence":["Compensation payments are important fiscal support, but they also show the Centre was still managing a strained post-GST settlement."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Appointment of Dr V Anantha Nageswaran as the Chief Economic Advisor","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-01-28","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1793311&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The government appointed Dr V. Anantha Nageswaran as Chief Economic Advisor, and he assumed charge on the same day.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official appointment notice naming the appointee and the position exactly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Modi government’s choice of senior economic personnel.","contrary_evidence":["One competent appointment does not by itself prove that all economic decisions were technocratic or evidence-based."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"NITI Aayog Welcomes Suman Bery as NITI Aayog Vice Chairman","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-05-01","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1821686&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"The government appointed economist Suman Bery as Vice Chairman of NITI Aayog, the Prime Minister’s policy think tank, effective May 1, 2022.","locator":"opening paragraph and quoted remarks","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official appointment release with clear identification of the office and the appointee’s background.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Modi as the head of government and NITI Aayog’s chair.","contrary_evidence":["NITI Aayog is advisory, so this says more about advice architecture than direct execution."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Economy | India | World Bank Human Capital","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"best available on page; accessed 2026-07-20","url":"https://humancapital.worldbank.org/en/economy/IND","claim":"The World Bank’s India human-capital page reports 2022 adult informal employment at 88.8 percent, youth unemployment at 17.8 percent, and female informal employment at 91.6 percent.","locator":"data table on the economy page","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank country page with explicit 2022 indicator values, but the page is dynamic and current-access rather than a contemporaneous 2022 publication.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Uses 2022 values even though the page is current.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level context only; not directly attributable to Modi without a policy link.","contrary_evidence":["This is a context indicator page, so it does not show which policy choices caused the labor-market structure."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Before global outlets paid attention, this small site had been digging into Adani Group for years despite lawsuits and threats","publisher":"Reuters Institute for the Study of Journalism","publication_date":"2023-02-07","url":"https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/news/global-outlets-paid-attention-small-site-had-been-digging-adani-group-years-despite-lawsuits","claim":"The Reuters Institute article said Gautam Adani was seen as a close aide to Prime Minister Narendra Modi and that the group had bagged massive government contracts in energy and big infrastructure development in recent years.","locator":"paragraphs discussing Adani’s proximity to Modi and government contracts","source_type":"scholarship/media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Well-sourced media-analysis piece, but it is retrospective and inferential rather than a direct finding of state favoritism.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective context for the Modi-era business environment, including 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect and contested; useful as capture context, not proof of a specific 2022 policy choice by Modi.","contrary_evidence":["The article is interpretive and does not by itself establish illegal favoritism or direct central-government order."],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.4"]}

Search and rejection summary
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across the budget, IMF, World Bank, RBI, PIB, PRS, GST, labor-market, and Reuters ecosystems. I rejected or collapsed:
- Duplicate monthly tax releases once one strong GST collection item and one GST-compensation item covered the fiscal pattern.
- Repeated budget pages that said the same thing as the PRS analysis without adding a new claim or clearer locator.
- Out-of-period 2023-2026 pages unless they were needed to report 2022 values or retrospectively describe 2022 reforms.
- Generic India macro pages that repeated the local WDI priors without ruler-specific policy attribution.
- Purely descriptive agency pages that did not add an actual policy choice, implementation measure, or outcome link.
- Press items about unrelated governance or foreign-policy questions that did not materially bear on economic well-being.
- Strongly duplicative coverage of PMGKAY, MGNREGS, and GatiShakti when one official announcement plus one independent synthesis already captured the point.

Remaining gaps
- 5B.5 is still the weakest lens. I have some capture-context evidence, but not a clean 2022 ruling-level record proving that Modi actively resisted favoritism, or the opposite, in a way that is directly attributable.
- 5B.2 is improved by the CEA and NITI Aayog appointments, but I do not yet have deep evidence on whether those advisers were actually empowered versus sidelined.
- Household-level distribution still needs better India-specific 2022 evidence on median consumption, regional gaps, and the extent to which growth reached poorer households.
- More search would help if you want direct evidence on business favoritism, procurement rules, or state support for connected conglomerates in 2022.
